# PostgreSQL whole-server restore

Restore the entire PostgreSQL Flexible Server from Azure automated
backups (PITR). Recovers all tenant databases to a point in time within
the 14-day retention window. PITR always restores to a **new** server.

Replace `<rg>`, `<id>`, `<postfix>`, `<user>`, `<tenant-app>`,
`<tenant-namespace>`, `<n>`, and timestamps before running.

## 1. Restore to a new server

bash
az postgres flexible-server restore \
  --resource-group <rg> \
  --name psql-<id>-<postfix>-restore \
  --source-server psql-<id>-<postfix> \
  --restore-time "2026-06-17T09:00:00Z"

Restore time (UTC) must be within the last 14 days. Old server stays live.

## 2. Validate the restored server

From a client pod inside the VNet (server is private):

bash
kubectl run pg-validate --rm -it --restart=Never --image=postgres:16-alpine -- /bin/sh

sh
export PGHOST="psql-<id>-<postfix>-restore.<...>.postgres.database.azure.com"
export PGPORT=5432 PGUSER="<user>" PGPASSWORD="<password>" PGSSLMODE="require"

psql -d postgres -c '\l'
psql -d "dbname=tenant_<uid>" -c '\dt'

Do not proceed until the data looks correct.

## 3. Stop writes to the old server

Skip this step if the old server is already dead (disaster). Otherwise
scale all tenant apps to 0 to avoid writing to both servers during
cutover (split-brain).

bash
kubectl scale deployment <tenant-app> --replicas=0 -n <tenant-namespace>

Downtime starts here.

## 4. Cut over

Point the connection config (FQDN / connection-string secret) at
`psql-<id>-<postfix>-restore` and redeploy. Then reconcile Terraform
state so the next `apply` does not revert the cutover.

## 5. Bring the app back up

bash
kubectl scale deployment <tenant-app> --replicas=<n> -n <tenant-namespace>

Downtime ends here.

## 6. Clean up

Keep the old server **stopped, not deleted** for a few days as a fallback.
Decommission once verified.

## Rollback

Old server is intact — repoint config back to `psql-<id>-<postfix>` and
restart:

bash
kubectl scale deployment <tenant-app> --replicas=0 -n <tenant-namespace>
# revert connection config to psql-<id>-<postfix>
kubectl scale deployment <tenant-app> --replicas=<n> -n <tenant-namespace>

## Notes

- All tenants restore to the same point in time; per-tenant restore is
  out of scope.
- Recent writes since the restore point are lost by design.
- Two servers run during cutover (~2x DB cost) until the old one is removed.
- Test in `app-development` first.
